 Native quota events use the same visible pool as downstream headers. HTTP/bridge/source SSE projects after raw upstream ingestion; native WebSocket forwarding resolves the existing aggregate cache after parsing and archival. Projection replaces rather than merges account fields. For example, accounts at 97 and 36 percent weekly usage emit 66.5 percent pooled usage. Unknown pools/families and hidden API-key quotas emit no event. Cache failures omit telemetry while inference continues; cancellation propagates. Public OpenAI streams retain vendor-event filtering. Raw account attribution and normal response lifecycle remain authoritative.
 
 See [requirements](spec.md) and community implementations [UP-PR-2561](https://github.com/Soju06/codex-lb/pull/2561) and [UP-PR-2308](https://github.com/Soju06/codex-lb/pull/2308).
+
+
+## Dead owners and late abandonment (2026-10-13)
+
+The [status-aware horizon contract](spec.md#requirement-dead-owner-recovery-horizons-reflect-the-owners-status) distinguishes quota resets from administrative or credential recovery. For example, a paused owner with a stored reset thirty seconds away cannot automatically return when that clock expires; conditional retirement releases its thread. A rate-limited or quota-exceeded owner with the same timestamp remains eligible for existing bounded waiting. This preserves stored status/reset metadata and the existing account lock and expected-owner fence.
+
+The [client-anchor contract](spec.md#requirement-proven-dead-client-anchors-receive-actionable-terminal-rejection) keeps the first and repeated client-anchored requests actionable: HTTP 404 bridge_previous_response_not_found asks for full history or a fresh conversation. A positive durable retirement marker is sufficient evidence even though ordinary lookup deliberately hides the former account. A genuinely missing owner retains its existing refusal. Uploaded files and encrypted reasoning retain their ownership constraints; an anchor-free eligible full resend can bind elsewhere.
+
+The [submit-time tombstone contract](spec.md#requirement-submit-time-abandonment-tombstones-retire-exact-proxy-anchors) handles a remote abandonment verdict arriving after planning. It retires the exact rejected live and durable carrier, preserves the initial 404 through outer recovery, and keeps the tombstone for delta-only refusal until replacement continuity succeeds. The undispatched request has no operation to reset for transparent replay. Successor anchors, client-supplied anchors, existing cleanup retry fences and cancellation ownership remain protected.
+
+The regression evidence uses disposable SQLite, synthetic upstream adapters, all public HTTP bridge paths and slash aliases, both native WebSocket paths and existing raw HTTP controls. Keyed tests enable real API-key enforcement, settle all four reservations and charge only the two successful turns. This does not certify live Desktop/provider behavior, native PostgreSQL/MySQL races, distributed rollout or hosted CI/production. Community reference implementations: [UP-PR-2423](https://github.com/Soju06/codex-lb/pull/2423) and [UP-PR-2467](https://github.com/Soju06/codex-lb/pull/2467).
